### What is the Longest Increasing Subsequence?

The LIS of a sequence is the longest subsequence where each element is strictly greater than the previous. Elements need not be contiguous. For example, in [3, 4, 1, 5], the LIS is [3, 4, 5] with length 3.

At the National Bank of India, a credit analyst reviews Rajesh's monthly balances and needs the longest streak of strictly increasing months to demonstrate consistent growth.

```java
package com.bank.credit;

import java.util.*;

public class LISDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] balances = {10000, 15000, 8000, 20000, 25000, 12000, 30000};
        System.out.println("LIS length: " + lisLength(balances));
        System.out.println("LIS months: " + lisReconstruct(balances));
    }

    // implementation below
}
```

### How does the quadratic tabulation work?

Create a length array where every entry starts at 1 (a single element is an LIS of length 1). For each index i, scan all earlier indices j. If nums[j] < nums[i] and len[j] + 1 > len[i], update len[i] and record j as the predecessor.

```java
package com.bank.credit;

public class LISTabulation {

    static int lisLength(int[] nums) {
        int n = nums.length;
        int[] len = new int[n];
        Arrays.fill(len, 1);
        int best = 1;

        for (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < i; j++) {
                if (nums[j] < nums[i] && len[j] + 1 > len[i]) {
                    len[i] = len[j] + 1;
                }
            }
            best = Math.max(best, len[i]);
        }
        return best;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] nums = {3, 4, 1, 5};
        System.out.println("LIS length: " + lisLength(nums));
    }
}
```

```text
LIS length: 3
```

### How do you reconstruct the actual subsequence?

Maintain a predecessor array prev[] initialized to -1. During the fill, when len[i] is updated from j, set prev[i] = j. After the fill, find the index with the best length. Walk backward from that index following prev[] links, collecting values, then reverse them.

```java
package com.bank.credit;

import java.util.*;

public class LISReconstruct {

    static List<Integer> lisReconstruct(int[] nums) {
        int n = nums.length;
        int[] len = new int[n];
        int[] prev = new int[n];
        Arrays.fill(len, 1);
        Arrays.fill(prev, -1);

        int bestLen = 1, bestIdx = 0;

        for (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < i; j++) {
                if (nums[j] < nums[i] && len[j] + 1 > len[i]) {
                    len[i] = len[j] + 1;
                    prev[i] = j;
                }
            }
            if (len[i] > bestLen) {
                bestLen = len[i];
                bestIdx = i;
            }
        }

        // Walk backward to reconstruct
        List<Integer> lis = new ArrayList<>();
        int idx = bestIdx;
        while (idx != -1) {
            lis.add(nums[idx]);
            idx = prev[idx];
        }
        Collections.reverse(lis);
        return lis;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] balances = {10000, 15000, 8000, 20000, 25000, 12000, 30000};
        List<Integer> lis = lisReconstruct(balances);
        System.out.println("LIS: " + lis);
        System.out.println("Length: " + lis.size());
    }
}
```

```text
LIS: [10000, 15000, 20000, 25000, 30000]
Length: 5
```

### How does the fill work step by step?

For balances [3, 4, 1, 5]:
- i=0: len[0]=1 (base case)
- i=1: j=0: nums[0]=3 < 4, len[0]+1=2 > 1 -> len[1]=2, prev[1]=0
- i=2: j=0: nums[0]=3 > 1, skip. j=1: nums[1]=4 > 1, skip. len[2]=1
- i=3: j=0: 3 < 5, len[0]+1=2. j=1: 4 < 5, len[1]+1=3 > 2 -> len[3]=3, prev[3]=1. j=2: 1 < 5, len[2]+1=2 < 3, skip.

```java
package com.bank.credit;

import java.util.Arrays;

public class LISStepByStep {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] nums = {3, 4, 1, 5};
        int n = nums.length;
        int[] len = new int[n];
        int[] prev = new int[n];
        Arrays.fill(len, 1);
        Arrays.fill(prev, -1);

        for (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < i; j++) {
                if (nums[j] < nums[i] && len[j] + 1 > len[i]) {
                    len[i] = len[j] + 1;
                    prev[i] = j;
                }
            }
            System.out.println("i=" + i + " len=" + Arrays.toString(len)
                + " prev=" + Arrays.toString(prev));
        }
    }
}
```

```text
i=1 len=[1, 2, 1, 1] prev=[-1, 0, -1, -1]
i=2 len=[1, 2, 1, 1] prev=[-1, 0, -1, -1]
i=3 len=[1, 2, 1, 3] prev=[-1, 0, -1, 1]
```

### How does this compare to the O(n log n) patience method?

The patience method uses binary search to maintain a tails array, achieving O(n log n) time. However, it only gives the LIS length directly. Reconstructing the actual subsequence requires extra bookkeeping. The quadratic method naturally supports reconstruction via the prev array.

| Method | Time | Space | Reconstruction |
|--------|------|-------|----------------|
| Quadratic tabulation | O(n^2) | O(n) | Natural via prev[] |
| Patience (binary search) | O(n log n) | O(n) | Extra bookkeeping needed |

```java
// Patience method (length only, O(n log n)):
int[] tails = new int[n];
int size = 0;
for (int num : nums) {
    int pos = Arrays.binarySearch(tails, 0, size, num);
    if (pos < 0) pos = -(pos + 1);
    tails[pos] = num;
    if (pos == size) size++;
}
// size is the LIS length
```

### What are the edge cases?

Edge cases include a single element (LIS = 1), a strictly increasing array (LIS = n), a strictly decreasing array (LIS = 1), and duplicate values (LIS is strictly increasing, so duplicates do not extend it).

```java
package com.bank.credit;

public class LISEdgeCases {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(lisLength(new int[]{5}));           // 1
        System.out.println(lisLength(new int[]{1, 2, 3, 4})); // 4
        System.out.println(lisLength(new int[]{4, 3, 2, 1})); // 1
        System.out.println(lisLength(new int[]{3, 3, 3}));     // 1 (strictly increasing)
    }

    static int lisLength(int[] nums) {
        int n = nums.length;
        int[] len = new int[n];
        java.util.Arrays.fill(len, 1);
        int best = 1;
        for (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < i; j++) {
                if (nums[j] < nums[i] && len[j] + 1 > len[i]) len[i] = len[j] + 1;
            }
            best = Math.max(best, len[i]);
        }
        return best;
    }
}
```

```text
1
4
1
1
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is forgetting to initialize every len[] entry to 1. Without this, the base case (single element = LIS of 1) is missing and all lengths would be 0.

```java
// WRONG: len[] defaults to 0
int[] len = new int[n];  // all zeros
// len[i] never starts at 1, so LIS of a single element would be 0

// CORRECT: initialize to 1
int[] len = new int[n];
Arrays.fill(len, 1);
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- **When to use quadratic vs patience.** Use the quadratic method when you need the actual subsequence or when n is small (under ~10,000). Use patience for large n when only the length is needed.
- **Non-strictly increasing.** For non-decreasing (allow equal), change the condition from `<` to `<=`. This matters for problems like Russian Doll Envelopes.
- **2D LIS.** The Longest Increasing Subsequence in 2D (e.g., sorting by one dimension and finding LIS in the other) is a common follow-up.
- **Real credit analysis.** In banking, the actual months matter more than the count, because the credit report must cite specific improving periods.
